process-loop-reductions.m's reducer spawned on the machine's GPU
(docs/INPROGRESS.md, GPU processes that run loops): its loops run across
every team of its module's kernel that no process uses, each thread with
partial results of the loops' reductions, which its team combines across
its waves and the process across the teams, from loops of one to a million
iterations.
REQUIRES: amdgpu-gpu

RUN: merkc -O2 -fopenmp -fdo-loops -fprocess-declarations --offload-arch=%offload-arch %libomp-flags %S/process-loop-reductions.m -o %t2
RUN: %gpu-env %t2 gpu | FileCheck %S/process-loop-reductions.m --match-full-lines --strict-whitespace

Without -fopenmp the directives are comments, and the reducer runs its loops
itself, one iteration after another.
RUN: merkc -O2 -fdo-loops -fprocess-declarations --offload-arch=%offload-arch %libomp-flags %S/process-loop-reductions.m -o %t.sequential
RUN: %gpu-env %t.sequential gpu | FileCheck %S/process-loop-reductions.m --match-full-lines --strict-whitespace

What the loops' bodies made goes with the reducer's arena when it ends.
RUN: %gpu-env MERK_CHECK_MESSAGES=1 MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY=1 %t2 gpu 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FREED
FREED: Mercury runtime: 0 messages are neither read nor freed
FREED-NEXT: Mercury runtime: 0 chunks of the devices' arenas and 0 rings are allocated
